.TH CTPOOL_TIMED_SUBMIT 3 "2026-10-01" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
ctpool_timed_submit \- submit a task with a timeout
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cthreadpool.h>

ccol_retval_t ctpool_timed_submit(ctpool pool,
                                  void (*fn)(void *),
                                  void *arg,
                                  void (*on_complete)(void *arg, bool ran),
                                  uint64_t timeout_us);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
The same as ctpool_submit, except that with a bounded queue it waits
at most timeout_us microseconds for a free slot, counted from the
call. The timeout is turned into an absolute deadline on
CLOCK_MONOTONIC, so a change to the system's wall clock (by an
administrator, by NTP, or when a virtual machine resumes) makes the
wait neither longer nor shorter.

A timeout_us of 0 makes the call the same as ctpool_try_submit, which
doesn't wait.

A timeout too large to form a deadline, such as UINT64_MAX, waits as
long as the queue stays full: the deadline saturates at the latest
time that can be represented and never wraps into the past.

A call from a task running on this same pool, or from the on_complete
callback of that task, never waits while a bounded queue is at
capacity, because only the workers of this pool can free a slot and
the caller is one of them. It returns ccol_container_full at once,
whatever timeout_us says.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
pool         Thread pool handle
fn           Task function (must not be NULL)
arg          The library gives it to fn and on_complete (may be NULL)
on_complete  Called once for the task: on_complete(arg, true) after
             fn returns, or on_complete(arg, false) when the pool
             discards the task unrun (may be NULL)
timeout_us   Longest time to wait, in microseconds (0 = do not wait)
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
ccol_success           - task enqueued.
ccol_invalid_args      - pool is CTPOOL_INVALID or a stale/already-
                         destroyed handle, or fn is NULL.
ccol_not_enough_memory - task struct allocation failed.
ccol_not_permitted     - pool is shutting down.
ccol_container_full    - the queue is full and timeout_us is 0, or the
                         caller is a task of this pool (see above).
ccol_timed_out         - timeout elapsed before a slot became free.
ccol_unexpected_failure - clock_gettime or pthread_cond_timedwait
                          returned an unexpected error.
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR ctpool_submit (3),
.BR ctpool_try_submit (3)
